id: question-packets/game-slots
title: "Game slots: seven questions a new Yawn asks by being empty"
kind: question-packet
record_shape: header
status: draft
version: 0.1.0-draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-13; the seven slots, their order, and the reason opponent and win are never cut are Dave's design for yawn.bot/{user}/new, restated; the leverage numbers and every seal are the agent's, not Dave's"
updated: 2026-09-13
runtime_surface: https://yawn.bot/new
loop_ref: core/articulation-floor.yawn
seal_schema_ref: schemas/articulation-floor.v0.1.schema.json

purpose: >
  Ask the orientation questions by showing the structure empty instead of
  composing a sentence. A new Yawn renders seven grey slots; each empty slot is
  a question and the label is the ask. The person fills slots by talking, and
  the surface reads their exact words into the slots and shows its work: which
  fragment filled which slot, who it is answerable to, and, for a slot the
  reader has not stated, the guess the bot sealed before they said it.

one_principle: >
  A filled slot is a proposition. An empty slot is a question. The same record
  holds both, so nothing has to be decided in advance about which the system
  stores: a slot is a question until it is filled and a proposition after.

relation_to_orientation_nine:
  rule: projection_of
  target: question-packets/orientation-nine.yawn
  note: >
    These are not a second ontology and not nine renamed keys. They are a
    compression of the orientation projection into the seven that tell a story
    about a game, for the one surface where a person arrives with nothing. Each
    slot names the orientation key it feeds; a fill is source for that key, not
    an answer to it. core/holarchy.yawn projection_of is the typed pointer.

projection_rule: >
  A surface may render these in any order, wording, or medium while
  question_key stays stable. Skipped slots stay empty and are lacunae, never
  filled to look complete. The reader's fill is an answer record; `you` stays
  null here and is filled per principal by their own records.

attribution_rule: >
  Every fill carries `by`. A fill the person stated is answerable to them and
  is `reported`. A fill the parser read from a cue is answerable to the parser,
  is `inferred`, and is sealed. Measured 2026-09-12 in this repository: 100 of
  194 records were last written by an agent identity, so a corpus parsed
  without `by` would return borrowed judgment as the principal's. The field is
  the guard against that, and it is why a dump of years of chat history can be
  read into slots at all.

seal_rule: >
  An inferred fill is a prediction of what the person would say, so it is
  sealed with lib/articulation-floor-v0.1.mjs at the moment the words arrived,
  blind, and revealed only when the person states that slot themselves. The
  reveal is scored hit, partial, or miss against their words. No seal, no
  inferred fill: a guess that can be edited after the answer is not a
  measurement.

next_move_rule: >
  The eighth row is derived, never authored: the highest-leverage empty slot,
  or, when an existing game matches the `game` slot, continuing there instead
  of splitting the map, or, when the seven are filled, the check that no other
  game is being played inside this one. Leverage below is declared, not
  measured; a floor from core/articulation-floor.yawn replaces it when one
  exists. Rank is not importance, truth, obligation, or permission
  (core/inquiry-selection.yawn).

questions:
  - question_key: game
    default_display_order: 1
    default_prompt: "What game is this?"
    leverage: 100
    basis: "No other slot means anything until the game is named."
    maps_to: [scope, intent]
    you: null

  - question_key: level
    default_display_order: 2
    default_prompt: "What level are you at in it?"
    leverage: 40
    basis: "Level 0 is the same for every game: still naming it. It closes last."
    maps_to: [current-state]
    you: null

  - question_key: arena
    default_display_order: 3
    default_prompt: "Where is it played?"
    leverage: 70
    basis: "The arena decides which rules and participants can apply at all."
    maps_to: [placement, boundary]
    you: null

  - question_key: participants
    default_display_order: 4
    default_prompt: "Who else is in this?"
    leverage: 60
    basis: "Participants fix who a move can reach and who can move back."
    maps_to: [perspective]
    you: null

  - question_key: opponent
    default_display_order: 5
    default_prompt: "What is in the way?"
    leverage: 90
    basis: "The opponent carries the narrative; without one there is no game, only a record."
    maps_to: [lacuna, boundary]
    you: null

  - question_key: rules
    default_display_order: 6
    default_prompt: "What are the rules on duty?"
    leverage: 50
    basis: "Rules say which standards apply while this game is running."
    maps_to: [boundary]
    you: null

  - question_key: win
    default_display_order: 7
    default_prompt: "What does winning look like?"
    leverage: 85
    basis: "Win is the other narrative slot: the condition the game exists to reach."
    maps_to: [movement, proof]
    you: null

lattice_note:
  claim: >
    Filled slots are attributes over records, so games split and group by the
    same operation: records sharing attributes form a tighter game, games
    sharing attributes roll up. The hierarchy is computed from the slots rather
    than authored.
  epistemic_status: inferred
  attributed_to: agent-on-behalf
  source_refs: [core/holarchy.yawn, question-packets/orientation-nine.yawn]
  not_implemented: >
    No lattice is computed anywhere in this repository or in the runtime today.
    The runtime matches a filled `game` slot against existing records by term
    overlap and offers to continue there. Writing this down is a claim about
    what the slots make possible, not a claim that it exists.

boundaries:
  - "a fill is source for an orientation key, never a ratified answer"
  - "an inferred fill is a proposal and stays inferred until the person states it"
  - "matching an existing game proposes navigation; moving an expression into it is the owner's press"
  - "nothing here activates a bot, spends, publishes, or writes outside the person's own record"

proof:
  source:
    - question-packets/orientation-nine.yawn
    - core/articulation-floor.yawn
    - core/inquiry-selection.yawn
    - lib/articulation-floor-v0.1.mjs
  covers:
    - questions
    - seal_rule
    - attribution_rule
  not_covered:
    - lattice_note
    - next_move_rule
  status: draft-backed

runtime_mirror:
  repository: "https://github.com/yawn-ai/yawn.bot"
  module: "src/domain/yawn-game-slots.ts"
  proof: "tests/unit/yawn-game-slots.test.ts"
  note: >
    The runtime mirror of this packet and its parser are proved in the product
    repository: the seven keys and their order, the seal hash recomputed with
    these exact bytes, the fragment offsets, and the reveal scoring. Those paths
    are in that tree, not this one, so they are named here rather than in proof:.
    Nothing in this tree executes the packet yet.
